00:05:57.428 --> 00:05:57.970
AUDIENCE: Hi.

00:05:57.970 --> 00:05:59.460
My name is Pablo.

00:05:59.460 --> 00:06:01.130
I have a question for you.

00:06:01.130 --> 00:06:03.870
What do you think about the
Bazel build system for Go?

00:06:10.878 --> 00:06:12.670
IAN TAYLOR: What do I
think about the Bazel

00:06:12.670 --> 00:06:13.540
build system for Go?

00:06:16.420 --> 00:06:19.780
I think it's very good
for complicated programs.

00:06:19.780 --> 00:06:21.340
Go comes with its
own build system,

00:06:21.340 --> 00:06:23.173
in the sense that it's
easy to build pure Go

00:06:23.173 --> 00:06:26.030
programs with the tools
that are provided by Go.

00:06:26.030 --> 00:06:29.987
But if you want to work on
a multi-language program

00:06:29.987 --> 00:06:31.570
with many different
parts, also if you

00:06:31.570 --> 00:06:33.680
want to deal with a
lot of generated code,

00:06:33.680 --> 00:06:37.960
then I think Bazel works
really effectively.

00:06:37.960 --> 00:06:40.300
It does have full support
for Go these days.

00:06:40.300 --> 00:06:43.940
And lots of people use it.

00:06:43.940 --> 00:06:44.960
We use it internally.

00:06:44.960 --> 00:06:46.900
We use Google's
internal version of it.

00:06:46.900 --> 00:06:48.280
So yeah.

00:06:48.280 --> 00:06:49.243
It's a good system.

00:06:49.243 --> 00:06:51.160
BRAD FITZPATRICK: You
say it has full support,

00:06:51.160 --> 00:06:53.020
but there's a lot of
things in X-tools,

00:06:53.020 --> 00:06:56.110
like the package loader and
stuff, that's in development.

00:06:56.110 --> 00:07:02.440
So a lot of the tools that
exist in the Go ecosystem

00:07:02.440 --> 00:07:05.200
historically assumed that you
laid out your code on disk,

00:07:05.200 --> 00:07:07.490
the way that Go's built-in
build system works.

00:07:07.490 --> 00:07:09.850
And so there's an
increasing amount of work

00:07:09.850 --> 00:07:12.130
recently about making
our tools agnostic,

00:07:12.130 --> 00:07:15.970
about whether you're using Bazel
or you're using the Go tool.

00:07:15.970 --> 00:07:18.985
So it's getting better,
only in the last maybe year.

00:07:18.985 --> 00:07:21.610
IAN TAYLOR: That's a good point,
especially for generated code.

00:07:24.650 --> 00:07:25.870
MEGAN SANICKI: OK, great.

00:07:25.870 --> 00:07:30.250
And I see we have another
question coming in

00:07:30.250 --> 00:07:33.820
AUDIENCE: Adding to what
Tyler was talking about,

00:07:33.820 --> 00:07:37.210
the benefits of Go, or
what it is good for,

00:07:37.210 --> 00:07:39.940
I think it's very good for
collaborative development,

00:07:39.940 --> 00:07:43.540
where it's so easy to import
somebody else's GitHub

00:07:43.540 --> 00:07:45.440
repo and their libraries.

00:07:45.440 --> 00:07:49.330
And Cobra is something
that almost everybody

00:07:49.330 --> 00:07:54.720
who develops some CLI or
the other might have used.

00:07:54.720 --> 00:08:00.160
So that's one thing that
I wanted to point out.

00:08:00.160 --> 00:08:03.520
My specific question
that I had was,

00:08:03.520 --> 00:08:09.670
is there plans of importing
commonly-used GitHub repos

00:08:09.670 --> 00:08:12.593
into the standard library?

00:08:12.593 --> 00:08:14.010
BRAD FITZPATRICK:
On the contrary.

00:08:14.010 --> 00:08:16.233
We want to get rid of things
in the standard library.

00:08:16.233 --> 00:08:17.650
We even have a FAQ
entry about it.

00:08:20.690 --> 00:08:22.690
Right now, stuff exists
in the standard library,

00:08:22.690 --> 00:08:26.162
just because initially,
there was no GoGet.

00:08:26.162 --> 00:08:28.120
And so if we wanted to
put some code somewhere,

00:08:28.120 --> 00:08:29.890
it had to be in the
standard library.

00:08:29.890 --> 00:08:31.690
There used to be
a Space Wars game.

00:08:31.690 --> 00:08:34.653
There was an NTP Usenet client.

00:08:34.653 --> 00:08:36.820
There was like tons of crap
in the standard library.

00:08:36.820 --> 00:08:39.700
And right before Go 1,
we deleted a ton of it.

00:08:39.700 --> 00:08:41.841
And we forgot to
delete even more of it.

00:08:41.841 --> 00:08:43.299
So there's a lot
of things that are

00:08:43.299 --> 00:08:45.130
in there that are an accident.

00:08:45.130 --> 00:08:49.120
And we didn't have internal
packages or private packages.

00:08:49.120 --> 00:08:52.270
So there's a lot of crap that's
in there, just for TLS, that's

00:08:52.270 --> 00:08:55.180
only public because of HTTP.

00:08:55.180 --> 00:08:58.060
And so there's a whole lot of
low-level crypto networking

00:08:58.060 --> 00:09:02.120
details that we wouldn't really
want to expose on their own.

00:09:02.120 --> 00:09:04.630
We might want them to live on
GitHub or something, anyway.

00:09:04.630 --> 00:09:07.160
So I don't think the
trend is to put more

00:09:07.160 --> 00:09:08.410
stuff in the standard library.

00:09:08.410 --> 00:09:10.787
It's really to make it
smaller and more modular.

00:09:10.787 --> 00:09:12.370
IAN TAYLOR: But I
want to add to that.

00:09:12.370 --> 00:09:16.780
With the Go modules work that's
going on actively in Go right

00:09:16.780 --> 00:09:20.140
now, we're making it
much easier for a program

00:09:20.140 --> 00:09:23.440
to pull in a reliably
determined set of sources

00:09:23.440 --> 00:09:25.130
from an external library.

00:09:25.130 --> 00:09:29.320
And we're also developing better
module discovery and package

00:09:29.320 --> 00:09:31.330
discovery tools so
that people will

00:09:31.330 --> 00:09:34.150
be able to find the
packages that they want.

00:09:34.150 --> 00:09:36.820
And also, have a way of
knowing that these are packages

00:09:36.820 --> 00:09:39.310
that a lot of other people
use and are therefore

00:09:39.310 --> 00:09:40.660
highly reliable.

00:09:40.660 --> 00:09:42.970
And it's tempting
to move everything

00:09:42.970 --> 00:09:44.510
into the standard library.

00:09:44.510 --> 00:09:46.360
But that doesn't solve
the problem of who's

00:09:46.360 --> 00:09:48.010
going to actually maintain it.

00:09:48.010 --> 00:09:50.530
The Go team is not
particularly large.

00:09:50.530 --> 00:09:52.120
And so we don't want
to put something

00:09:52.120 --> 00:09:55.190
in the standard library which
we can't take care of ourselves.

00:09:55.190 --> 00:09:57.340
But what we do instead
want to make it possible

00:09:57.340 --> 00:09:59.530
is that you can
build your Go program

00:09:59.530 --> 00:10:01.610
and know that you're
pulling in libraries written

00:10:01.610 --> 00:10:03.235
and maintained by
other people that you

00:10:03.235 --> 00:10:06.340
know are high quality because
other people vouch for them.

00:10:06.340 --> 00:10:09.700
That's the direction
we want to move in.

00:10:09.700 --> 00:10:10.960
MEGAN SANICKI: That's great.

00:10:10.960 --> 00:10:13.580
OK, we have another
question over here.

00:10:13.580 --> 00:10:14.560
AUDIENCE: Hi.

00:10:14.560 --> 00:10:17.080
My question is for Tyler.

00:10:17.080 --> 00:10:23.140
Seems that Go have been launched
near about 10 years ago, as

00:10:23.140 --> 00:10:27.170
well as Google Cloud
about 10 years ago.

00:10:27.170 --> 00:10:29.470
But from my point
of view, support

00:10:29.470 --> 00:10:34.620
of Go in Google Cloud and Google
App Engine flexible environment

00:10:34.620 --> 00:10:39.301
still looks insufficient
from my point of view.

00:10:39.301 --> 00:10:41.200
Are you guys prefer
Java, aren't you?

00:10:44.257 --> 00:10:45.340
TYLER BUI-PALSULICH: Yeah.

00:10:45.340 --> 00:10:47.132
I think this is something
that we've really

00:10:47.132 --> 00:10:50.433
tried to improve in the
last couple of years.

00:10:50.433 --> 00:10:51.850
So a couple of
things that are new

00:10:51.850 --> 00:10:55.750
are App Engine standard
second-gen run times.

00:10:55.750 --> 00:10:59.860
So this idea applies for
all of the App Engine

00:10:59.860 --> 00:11:01.150
supported languages.

00:11:01.150 --> 00:11:03.940
But specifically for
Go, if you ever used

00:11:03.940 --> 00:11:07.430
App Engine standard before, and
you had to import App Engine--

00:11:07.430 --> 00:11:09.560
anyone, raise your hand, used?

00:11:09.560 --> 00:11:11.110
A couple people.

00:11:11.110 --> 00:11:15.640
So now you can use
whatever library you want.

00:11:15.640 --> 00:11:18.520
Before, one of the issues
that would come up is,

00:11:18.520 --> 00:11:21.850
you had to use a special
package to make HTTP requests.

00:11:21.850 --> 00:11:24.280
So you run your
service on App Engine.

00:11:24.280 --> 00:11:25.930
You need to make some API call.

00:11:25.930 --> 00:11:28.540
You can't use net HTTP.

00:11:28.540 --> 00:11:32.440
You need to use URL Fetch, which
was this other package that we

00:11:32.440 --> 00:11:33.640
maintained.

00:11:33.640 --> 00:11:36.190
Now latest version
of Go, that's not

00:11:36.190 --> 00:11:37.750
something you need
to do anymore.

00:11:37.750 --> 00:11:39.570
Much, much nicer to use.

00:11:39.570 --> 00:11:41.740
And that's all based on gvisor.

00:11:41.740 --> 00:11:44.920
That same technology was
used for Cloud Functions.

00:11:44.920 --> 00:11:49.330
And so we released Cloud
Function support for Go

00:11:49.330 --> 00:11:52.630
about a year ago, I
want to say, starting

00:11:52.630 --> 00:11:54.880
with an alpha and beta.

00:11:54.880 --> 00:11:59.740
And Go 111 for Cloud
Functions just went to GA.

00:11:59.740 --> 00:12:01.750
So those are some of
the ways that we've

00:12:01.750 --> 00:12:03.280
tried to improve support.

00:12:03.280 --> 00:12:07.480
And then the one other way to
run your Go code is Cloud Run.

00:12:07.480 --> 00:12:09.880
And you can run whatever
language you want,

00:12:09.880 --> 00:12:11.750
whatever language
version you want.

00:12:11.750 --> 00:12:14.140
And as long as you're using
the cloud client libraries,

00:12:14.140 --> 00:12:16.720
hopefully, that's
a nice experience.

00:12:16.720 --> 00:12:19.930
And then you mentioned
App Engine Flex.

00:12:19.930 --> 00:12:21.540
For someone who
hasn't used it, it's

00:12:21.540 --> 00:12:23.830
similar to App Engine standard.

00:12:23.830 --> 00:12:26.320
You can also provide
your own container images

00:12:26.320 --> 00:12:27.850
that you want to run.

00:12:27.850 --> 00:12:29.080
It will scale up for you.

00:12:29.080 --> 00:12:33.040
But it has a minimum
instance count of 1.

00:12:33.040 --> 00:12:34.660
That's the default level.

00:12:34.660 --> 00:12:38.437
And then if you're comparing
that to Cloud Run, which--

00:12:38.437 --> 00:12:40.020
how many people saw
that announcement?

00:12:40.020 --> 00:12:41.740
It was in the keynote today.

00:12:41.740 --> 00:12:42.490
A lot more people.

00:12:42.490 --> 00:12:45.540
So Cloud Run is
serverless containers.

00:12:45.540 --> 00:12:47.880
So you define whatever
container image you want.

00:12:47.880 --> 00:12:50.940
And that can bundle whatever
Go version you want.

00:12:50.940 --> 00:12:52.960
And then that scales up
and down really quickly.

00:12:52.960 --> 00:12:55.420
If you deploy, it literally
takes a few seconds.

00:12:55.420 --> 00:12:58.600
It's really, really nice.

00:12:58.600 --> 00:12:59.830
So we're working on it.

00:12:59.830 --> 00:13:04.400
I hope it's much better than
it was, say, 3, 5 years ago.

00:13:04.400 --> 00:13:05.900
ROBERT VAN GENT:
And I wanted to put

00:13:05.900 --> 00:13:08.540
in a plug for my project,
the Go Cloud Development Kit.

00:13:08.540 --> 00:13:10.810
We're another open
source project.

00:13:10.810 --> 00:13:13.510
And we're trying to make Go a
great language for developing

00:13:13.510 --> 00:13:17.650
cloud applications, not just on
GCP but on any cloud, including

00:13:17.650 --> 00:13:20.810
on-prem or running
on your local box.

00:13:20.810 --> 00:13:22.660
So we've developed a
bunch of portable APIs

00:13:22.660 --> 00:13:26.450
for cloud services, like Blob
or Pubsub, things like that.

00:13:26.450 --> 00:13:29.370
And then we have implementations
for different cloud providers.

00:13:29.370 --> 00:13:34.090
So you write your app once
using our APIs, native Go APIs.

00:13:34.090 --> 00:13:38.900
And then you plug-in with simple
config or initialization time

00:13:38.900 --> 00:13:39.400
changes.

00:13:39.400 --> 00:13:42.460
You plug in whatever
implementation you want.

00:13:42.460 --> 00:13:44.243
If you want to run
locally on your box,

00:13:44.243 --> 00:13:45.410
you use that implementation.

00:13:45.410 --> 00:13:48.860
If you want to run on
Amazon, you plug in S3 or SNS

00:13:48.860 --> 00:13:51.140
for Pubsub.

00:13:51.140 --> 00:13:53.260
And so that way,
you have flexibility

00:13:53.260 --> 00:13:54.760
and good development workflow.

00:13:58.760 --> 00:14:00.260
MEGAN SANICKI:
Anything else to add?

00:14:03.860 --> 00:14:07.280
If not, we do have a
question coming in.

00:14:07.280 --> 00:14:11.120
AUDIENCE: I just have a question
with regards to WebAssembly.

00:14:11.120 --> 00:14:14.240
What is the roadmap
for integrating

00:14:14.240 --> 00:14:16.700
Go with WebAssembly?

00:14:16.700 --> 00:14:19.760
Will there be one day where
we can do away with JavaScript

00:14:19.760 --> 00:14:23.240
and have Go replace it?

00:14:23.240 --> 00:14:24.600
BRAD FITZPATRICK: I hope so.

00:14:24.600 --> 00:14:27.050
But it's getting better.

00:14:27.050 --> 00:14:29.865
None of us are really doing
the WebAssembly back end.

00:14:29.865 --> 00:14:32.240
There's some people on the Go
team that are reviewing it.

00:14:32.240 --> 00:14:35.432
But it's mostly done from
external contributions.

00:14:35.432 --> 00:14:36.890
There was just a
change that landed

00:14:36.890 --> 00:14:39.735
that made it smaller
and faster, the output.

00:14:39.735 --> 00:14:42.043
IAN TAYLOR: Why don't you
say what the status is today?

00:14:42.043 --> 00:14:43.460
BRAD FITZPATRICK:
The status today

00:14:43.460 --> 00:14:45.918
is, you can take programs and
compile them for WebAssembly.

00:14:45.918 --> 00:14:48.432
And you could run it
alongside Node.js stuff.

00:14:48.432 --> 00:14:49.890
Or you could run
it in the browser.

00:14:49.890 --> 00:14:54.260
And there's an ecosystem
of UI framework stuff

00:14:54.260 --> 00:14:55.760
so you can write
front end web apps.

00:14:55.760 --> 00:14:58.400
And you can write it all today
without any JavaScript at all.

00:14:58.400 --> 00:15:04.070
So it's nascent, but
it's coming along.

00:15:04.070 --> 00:15:06.380
MEGAN SANICKI: And actually,
I just want to see--

00:15:06.380 --> 00:15:08.797
we're hearing a little bit
through these questions, what's

00:15:08.797 --> 00:15:12.260
new with modules and
other aspects, functions.

00:15:12.260 --> 00:15:14.510
Could you just tell
us, in one big arcing

00:15:14.510 --> 00:15:16.150
story, what is new with Go?

00:15:16.150 --> 00:15:17.720
There's just a lot happening.

00:15:21.493 --> 00:15:22.910
ROBERT GRIESEMER:
Well, I can talk

00:15:22.910 --> 00:15:24.870
a little bit about the
changes to the language.

00:15:24.870 --> 00:15:28.340
So until the most
recent release,

00:15:28.340 --> 00:15:33.590
to 112, we have made almost
no changes to the language

00:15:33.590 --> 00:15:37.070
after an initial phase where
we made small, minor changes.

00:15:37.070 --> 00:15:39.290
So for the last couple
of years, we basically

00:15:39.290 --> 00:15:41.000
have frozen the
language, even when

00:15:41.000 --> 00:15:44.660
it comes to small, minor
adjustments that would have

00:15:44.660 --> 00:15:46.340
been fully backward compatible.

00:15:46.340 --> 00:15:48.350
But now we're trying
to move forward

00:15:48.350 --> 00:15:54.440
towards what we call Go 2
And as part of this process,

00:15:54.440 --> 00:15:58.070
we have started to look at the
incoming proposals that come

00:15:58.070 --> 00:16:00.020
from the open source community.

00:16:00.020 --> 00:16:03.150
And we review them
and we identify

00:16:03.150 --> 00:16:04.670
the ones that seem promising.

00:16:04.670 --> 00:16:06.980
And so for 113, for
instance, we have

00:16:06.980 --> 00:16:09.703
started making the first
significant changes

00:16:09.703 --> 00:16:10.370
to the language.

00:16:10.370 --> 00:16:13.700
So now you will have,
with 113, assuming

00:16:13.700 --> 00:16:16.370
we're going to actually
say yes at the very end

00:16:16.370 --> 00:16:19.920
of the development cycle,
of binary literals.

00:16:19.920 --> 00:16:23.430
That's going to be hexadecimal
floating point numbers.

00:16:23.430 --> 00:16:27.830
Another thing is, we can
do now non-constant shifts

00:16:27.830 --> 00:16:31.532
without uint conversion,
which is probably going

00:16:31.532 --> 00:16:32.615
to clean up a lot of code.

00:16:37.400 --> 00:16:39.650
And literals can
have under bars.

00:16:39.650 --> 00:16:41.240
So if you have
very long numbers,

00:16:41.240 --> 00:16:45.020
you can have now underscores
in there to space them out.

00:16:45.020 --> 00:16:47.280
Apparently, something
that people really wanted.

00:16:47.280 --> 00:16:49.970
And there is
probably going to be

00:16:49.970 --> 00:16:51.650
some changes to the
libraries, which

00:16:51.650 --> 00:16:55.573
I probably not have the
best overview at the moment.

00:16:55.573 --> 00:16:56.990
BRAD FITZPATRICK:
I was also going

00:16:56.990 --> 00:16:59.240
to add that all these language
changes that are coming

00:16:59.240 --> 00:17:02.342
are like warm-up ones for
us to work on the process.

00:17:02.342 --> 00:17:04.550
So they're kind of boring,
and they're kind of small,

00:17:04.550 --> 00:17:07.625
but we're going to do a couple
additions to the language.

00:17:07.625 --> 00:17:09.750
We're going to do a couple
removals to the language

00:17:09.750 --> 00:17:11.843
and get practice
with that process.

00:17:11.843 --> 00:17:13.760
There's a couple of
constructs in the language

00:17:13.760 --> 00:17:16.310
that we would like
to maybe remove.

00:17:16.310 --> 00:17:21.800
And so modules lets you specify,
on the granularity of a module,

00:17:21.800 --> 00:17:24.270
what language version
semantics you want.

00:17:24.270 --> 00:17:27.589
So if you say you're
targeting Go 112,

00:17:27.589 --> 00:17:29.450
we can remove
something and Go 113.

00:17:29.450 --> 00:17:31.910
As long as your module
still declares 112,

00:17:31.910 --> 00:17:33.170
that language feature exists.

00:17:33.170 --> 00:17:36.020
But once you want some
new feature in Go 114,

00:17:36.020 --> 00:17:38.030
and you say that, then
you lose the features

00:17:38.030 --> 00:17:39.590
that we removed in Go 113.

00:17:39.590 --> 00:17:42.530
So it lets us slowly
evolve the language

00:17:42.530 --> 00:17:43.760
without breaking anyone.

00:17:43.760 --> 00:17:46.290
So there may not
actually be a Go 2 ever.

00:17:46.290 --> 00:17:49.100
But we say Go 2,
as in new stuff.

00:17:49.100 --> 00:17:51.650
But it may just be that
generics or something fun

00:17:51.650 --> 00:17:54.830
lands in Go 117.

00:17:54.830 --> 00:17:55.850
So keep that in mind.

00:17:55.850 --> 00:17:57.650
Whenever we say Go
2, it's probably

00:17:57.650 --> 00:18:01.932
more of a marketing expression
than actually a thing.

00:18:01.932 --> 00:18:03.640
TYLER BUI-PALSULICH:
And just to call out

00:18:03.640 --> 00:18:07.330
the three main things being
looked at by the team for Go 2

00:18:07.330 --> 00:18:09.250
are dependency management.

00:18:09.250 --> 00:18:12.340
So modules are the
Go solution to that.

00:18:12.340 --> 00:18:15.130
Came out starting in 111.

00:18:15.130 --> 00:18:17.110
Now again in 112,
and then hopefully,

00:18:17.110 --> 00:18:19.590
on by default in 113.

00:18:19.590 --> 00:18:22.600
And then the others are
errors and generics.

00:18:22.600 --> 00:18:25.480
So errors, the goal there
is to make it easier

00:18:25.480 --> 00:18:30.910
to develop and respond
to and check errors.

00:18:30.910 --> 00:18:35.140
And then last one, generics,
of basically deciding, should

00:18:35.140 --> 00:18:35.890
we include them?

00:18:35.890 --> 00:18:36.710
Should we not?

00:18:36.710 --> 00:18:38.140
If we do, in what form?

00:18:40.705 --> 00:18:41.580
MEGAN SANICKI: Great.

00:18:41.580 --> 00:18:43.170
Thank you.

00:18:43.170 --> 00:18:44.930
I think we have a question.

00:18:44.930 --> 00:18:48.100
AUDIENCE: Brad actually just
answered my original question.

00:18:48.100 --> 00:18:49.930
So a different question.

00:18:49.930 --> 00:18:52.530
I think one of Go's
strengths, as you mentioned,

00:18:52.530 --> 00:18:54.210
is how few features it has.

00:18:57.183 --> 00:18:59.600
Could you give an example of
a feature in another language

00:18:59.600 --> 00:19:01.810
that you really like that
wouldn't make sense in Go?

00:19:10.350 --> 00:19:12.040
MEGAN SANICKI: That
was a good one.

00:19:12.040 --> 00:19:12.790
You get a plushie.

00:19:16.750 --> 00:19:19.450
IAN TAYLOR: Well,
C++, for example,

00:19:19.450 --> 00:19:22.830
is a very complex language.

00:19:22.830 --> 00:19:25.170
But it has a number of
features that are very nice.

00:19:28.050 --> 00:19:33.480
For example, in C++, you can
templatize based on a constant

00:19:33.480 --> 00:19:40.500
and use that to compile
different versions of your code

00:19:40.500 --> 00:19:43.180
based on constants that are
passed into the template.

00:19:43.180 --> 00:19:45.510
And you can combine that
with variadic templates.

00:19:45.510 --> 00:19:49.380
So you can actually redesign
your data structures

00:19:49.380 --> 00:19:52.140
based on how the
template is expanded

00:19:52.140 --> 00:19:54.270
to make them more
or less efficient,

00:19:54.270 --> 00:19:55.832
depending on the
exact features you

00:19:55.832 --> 00:19:57.040
need from the data structure.

00:19:57.040 --> 00:19:58.710
You can actually
change how much memory

00:19:58.710 --> 00:20:01.720
it requires at compile time.

00:20:01.720 --> 00:20:03.390
So that's a cool feature.

00:20:03.390 --> 00:20:06.510
And I've used that feature
myself when I've written in C++

00:20:06.510 --> 00:20:07.710
in the past.

00:20:07.710 --> 00:20:10.590
But the complexity
of understanding

00:20:10.590 --> 00:20:12.400
how that feature really works.

00:20:12.400 --> 00:20:14.340
I don't think it's appropriate
for a language like Go, which

00:20:14.340 --> 00:20:15.570
is designed to be--

00:20:15.570 --> 00:20:19.050
as Robert said earlier,
it's a language

00:20:19.050 --> 00:20:20.700
you can learn very
quickly if you're

00:20:20.700 --> 00:20:22.170
familiar with other
programming languages.

00:20:22.170 --> 00:20:24.378
And even if you're not
familiar with other languages,

00:20:24.378 --> 00:20:26.050
it's a great language
to start with.

00:20:26.050 --> 00:20:28.500
We don't want features
like that, I think, in Go.

00:20:28.500 --> 00:20:31.237
But it's a great
question, though.

00:20:31.237 --> 00:20:32.820
BRAD FITZPATRICK:
In general, Go tries

00:20:32.820 --> 00:20:37.440
to give you 80% of what you
want with 20% of the complexity.

00:20:37.440 --> 00:20:39.670
Every time you try to
solve a little bit more

00:20:39.670 --> 00:20:42.870
or get it closer to 100% of what
you want, the complexity just

00:20:42.870 --> 00:20:46.098
ramps up way faster than
what you're delivering.

00:20:46.098 --> 00:20:47.640
ROBERT GRIESEMER:
There's one feature

00:20:47.640 --> 00:20:50.530
that's relatively easy
to understand, I think,

00:20:50.530 --> 00:20:53.200
which is macros,
which Go doesn't have.

00:20:53.200 --> 00:20:57.390
But there's plenty of times when
I really, really, really wanted

00:20:57.390 --> 00:20:59.020
to just write a macro.

00:20:59.020 --> 00:21:02.100
But the problem with
a macro is, it's

00:21:02.100 --> 00:21:04.830
possible to write
code that's almost

00:21:04.830 --> 00:21:06.190
indecipherable afterwards.

00:21:06.190 --> 00:21:07.190
So it's easy.

00:21:07.190 --> 00:21:10.640
It's like, write once,
understand never.

00:21:10.640 --> 00:21:15.038
So it's a feature
that definitely

00:21:15.038 --> 00:21:16.830
that we don't want to
have in the language,

00:21:16.830 --> 00:21:20.520
because we put a lot of
emphasis on readability of Go.

00:21:20.520 --> 00:21:23.190
Because code, in our
experience, is read

00:21:23.190 --> 00:21:24.620
much more often than written.

00:21:24.620 --> 00:21:27.405
And so that's a valuable
feature to maintain.

00:21:32.140 --> 00:21:33.560
MEGAN SANICKI: Great.

00:21:33.560 --> 00:21:37.180
I think we have another
question coming in.

00:21:37.180 --> 00:21:39.640
AUDIENCE: So Go is
about 10 years old now.

00:21:39.640 --> 00:21:42.400
So if you can comment
a little bit about,

00:21:42.400 --> 00:21:44.710
what were the
motivations that drove

00:21:44.710 --> 00:21:46.870
you to develop Go 10 years ago?

00:21:46.870 --> 00:21:48.850
And are these drivers
still valid today?

00:21:51.775 --> 00:21:53.400
ROBERT GRIESEMER: So
this is a question

00:21:53.400 --> 00:21:55.470
that's been answered
many times on the web.

00:21:55.470 --> 00:21:59.350
But let me try to shortcut it.

00:21:59.350 --> 00:22:03.312
So in 2007, there was a lot of
code to be written at Google.

00:22:03.312 --> 00:22:05.520
Still, there's a lot of code
to be written at Google.

00:22:05.520 --> 00:22:08.682
And some of us really
felt like at that time,

00:22:08.682 --> 00:22:10.140
the choice of
programming languages

00:22:10.140 --> 00:22:12.580
was somewhat unsatisfactory.

00:22:12.580 --> 00:22:14.580
So we were somewhat
stuck between a rock

00:22:14.580 --> 00:22:16.170
and a hard place, if you will.

00:22:16.170 --> 00:22:17.895
Some people called
it Java and C++.

00:22:20.940 --> 00:22:26.590
So there was a strong desire
to have something better.

00:22:26.590 --> 00:22:30.480
And so we embarked on
coming up with something

00:22:30.480 --> 00:22:33.150
that felt like a much
better solution at that time

00:22:33.150 --> 00:22:34.980
to the kind of
systems programming

00:22:34.980 --> 00:22:36.450
that we wanted to do.

00:22:36.450 --> 00:22:40.260
Because it seemed that
a lot of the problems

00:22:40.260 --> 00:22:43.110
were actually solved many,
many years, and maybe

00:22:43.110 --> 00:22:46.590
decades before, but
were not actually

00:22:46.590 --> 00:22:48.120
solved in programming
languages that

00:22:48.120 --> 00:22:50.560
were available at that time.

00:22:50.560 --> 00:22:53.610
And so I think a
lot of those things

00:22:53.610 --> 00:22:55.680
are still maybe
a little bit less

00:22:55.680 --> 00:22:57.180
true today, because
there's actually

00:22:57.180 --> 00:22:59.130
a wider variety of languages.

00:22:59.130 --> 00:23:01.080
But when it comes to
things like concurrency,

00:23:01.080 --> 00:23:06.113
I think Go is still
way in the forefront.

00:23:06.113 --> 00:23:07.530
I don't know of
any other language

00:23:07.530 --> 00:23:10.050
where it's so easy to
write concurrent code.

00:23:10.050 --> 00:23:12.210
In terms of compactness,
I think Go is still

00:23:12.210 --> 00:23:15.750
extremely small
language and very easy

00:23:15.750 --> 00:23:18.490
to grasp and understand.

00:23:18.490 --> 00:23:20.610
So I think many of these
things are still valid.

00:23:20.610 --> 00:23:23.610
And, of course,
the language is not

00:23:23.610 --> 00:23:25.380
just compact and
small and clean,

00:23:25.380 --> 00:23:26.680
but it's also very efficient.

00:23:26.680 --> 00:23:27.990
And I think that's still true.

00:23:27.990 --> 00:23:32.610
We're maybe not
at C or C++ level,

00:23:32.610 --> 00:23:40.458
but we are close enough for
99% of all applications.

00:23:40.458 --> 00:23:43.000
IAN TAYLOR: And I want to also
allude to something that Tyler

00:23:43.000 --> 00:23:45.730
mentioned earlier, which
is that Go the language

00:23:45.730 --> 00:23:47.740
was designed, as
much as possible,

00:23:47.740 --> 00:23:52.240
for programming in the large,
for many people working

00:23:52.240 --> 00:23:54.372
together on one program.

00:23:54.372 --> 00:23:56.080
And a lot of decisions
about the language

00:23:56.080 --> 00:23:58.120
were made with that in mind.

00:23:58.120 --> 00:24:01.580
How well we succeeded
at it, I'm not sure.

00:24:01.580 --> 00:24:04.330
But I think it was an
important goal at the time.

00:24:04.330 --> 00:24:06.130
And I think it's even
more important now,

00:24:06.130 --> 00:24:08.320
especially as open source
has continued to get

00:24:08.320 --> 00:24:10.780
larger and larger and larger.

00:24:10.780 --> 00:24:16.510
Languages that help people
work in larger groups, I think,

00:24:16.510 --> 00:24:19.570
is a very important goal
for modern languages.

00:24:19.570 --> 00:24:24.665
And I think Go has aimed
for that all along.

00:24:24.665 --> 00:24:25.790
MEGAN SANICKI: That's good.

00:24:25.790 --> 00:24:27.820
Anything else?

00:24:27.820 --> 00:24:30.392
Well, I know we have a very
patient person over here who's

00:24:30.392 --> 00:24:31.475
waiting to ask a question.

00:24:37.847 --> 00:24:40.180
AUDIENCE: One of the things
I've really enjoyed about Go

00:24:40.180 --> 00:24:41.200
is the community.

00:24:41.200 --> 00:24:44.470
And I'm wondering how you all
stay engaged with the community

00:24:44.470 --> 00:24:47.080
and help build that,
especially in the face

00:24:47.080 --> 00:24:51.650
of contentious decisions
or changes to the language.

00:24:51.650 --> 00:24:53.830
IAN TAYLOR: So the question
is about the community

00:24:53.830 --> 00:24:57.260
and how we work
with it, I guess.

00:24:57.260 --> 00:24:57.760
Yeah.

00:24:57.760 --> 00:25:01.010
The community for Go
has been incredible.

00:25:01.010 --> 00:25:05.890
We've had so much support
from all of you, from people

00:25:05.890 --> 00:25:08.920
all around the world, working
in Go and contributing to Go.

00:25:08.920 --> 00:25:10.930
And then you asked,
how do we deal

00:25:10.930 --> 00:25:12.310
with contentious decisions?

00:25:15.520 --> 00:25:17.530
I think we try to be
as open as possible

00:25:17.530 --> 00:25:19.472
about how we're
making our decisions.

00:25:19.472 --> 00:25:21.430
And I shouldn't even say
as open it's possible.

00:25:21.430 --> 00:25:26.110
We try to be completely open
about why we're deciding

00:25:26.110 --> 00:25:27.160
the way we're deciding.

00:25:27.160 --> 00:25:30.100
And things do get
contentious at times.

00:25:30.100 --> 00:25:32.620
There are disagreements in
the community, of course.

00:25:32.620 --> 00:25:34.510
There have been
disagreements in the past.

00:25:34.510 --> 00:25:37.330
And we listen to them.

00:25:37.330 --> 00:25:41.320
An easy example is
for the product--

00:25:41.320 --> 00:25:45.160
the language added a
feature, type aliases,

00:25:45.160 --> 00:25:46.870
maybe three, four years ago.

00:25:46.870 --> 00:25:48.680
And before type aliases
were introduced,

00:25:48.680 --> 00:25:50.180
we actually had a
separate proposal,

00:25:50.180 --> 00:25:52.540
which was a different
kind of aliasing proposal.

00:25:52.540 --> 00:25:54.640
And we designed it.

00:25:54.640 --> 00:25:57.790
Robert and I both worked on it,
and other people within Google.

00:25:57.790 --> 00:25:59.380
We put it out there
saying, this is

00:25:59.380 --> 00:26:01.253
something we think will
be useful that we'd

00:26:01.253 --> 00:26:02.420
like to add to the language.

00:26:02.420 --> 00:26:05.230
And there was a lot of
push back in the community.

00:26:05.230 --> 00:26:06.400
It did become contentious.

00:26:06.400 --> 00:26:10.060
And I think we tried as hard
as we could to listen to it.

00:26:10.060 --> 00:26:12.700
We withdrew the proposal.

00:26:12.700 --> 00:26:16.510
We put out a smaller and
simpler proposal, which did then

00:26:16.510 --> 00:26:17.650
get added to the language.

00:26:17.650 --> 00:26:21.800
Also, we explained
the use cases for it.

00:26:21.800 --> 00:26:26.470
And so I think it's been a
learning process for all of us

00:26:26.470 --> 00:26:28.040
to work with the community.

00:26:28.040 --> 00:26:32.320
But I feel like the answer
really is just to be open,

00:26:32.320 --> 00:26:34.930
to explain why we're
thinking what we're thinking.

00:26:34.930 --> 00:26:36.715
We can't take every
idea that comes in.

00:26:36.715 --> 00:26:38.590
We don't agree with
every idea that comes in.

00:26:38.590 --> 00:26:42.240
Ultimately, a small
group of people

00:26:42.240 --> 00:26:44.883
is going to make the very
final decisions about what's

00:26:44.883 --> 00:26:45.550
going to happen.

00:26:45.550 --> 00:26:46.972
But still, most
of the best ideas

00:26:46.972 --> 00:26:48.055
have come in from outside.

00:26:53.913 --> 00:26:55.080
MEGAN SANICKI: That's great.

00:26:55.080 --> 00:26:57.010
Being open is important.

00:26:57.010 --> 00:26:59.050
Any other comments on that one?

00:26:59.050 --> 00:26:59.550
OK.

00:26:59.550 --> 00:27:02.008
Then I'm going take a
question on this side.

00:27:02.008 --> 00:27:02.550
AUDIENCE: Hi.

00:27:02.550 --> 00:27:05.130
So you guys are great at keeping
stuff backwards compatible.

00:27:05.130 --> 00:27:08.305
But if you could go tomorrow to
work and just change something

00:27:08.305 --> 00:27:10.680
completely without thinking
about it, what would that be?

00:27:16.770 --> 00:27:18.770
ROBERT GRIESEMER: If you
can go back all the way

00:27:18.770 --> 00:27:20.600
to the beginning of
Go, the original Go

00:27:20.600 --> 00:27:23.540
looked very, very different
than the one that we have now.

00:27:23.540 --> 00:27:25.333
And in the early days,
we had the luxury

00:27:25.333 --> 00:27:26.750
to actually make
a lot of changes.

00:27:26.750 --> 00:27:28.542
For instance, the very
first version of Go,

00:27:28.542 --> 00:27:29.915
you had to write semicolons.

00:27:29.915 --> 00:27:31.790
And now you don't have
to write them anymore.

00:27:31.790 --> 00:27:35.462
Actually, for last 10 years,
you didn't have to write them.

00:27:35.462 --> 00:27:36.920
And there's a whole
bunch of things

00:27:36.920 --> 00:27:39.087
that we probably would like
to remove, some of which

00:27:39.087 --> 00:27:41.430
we can't remove right now.

00:27:41.430 --> 00:27:43.700
One of them, again,
a simple example

00:27:43.700 --> 00:27:48.660
is, right now, you can
say string of an integer.

00:27:48.660 --> 00:27:52.790
And that gives you
the corresponding--

00:27:52.790 --> 00:27:55.640
it doesn't give you a string
version of that integer value.

00:27:55.640 --> 00:27:58.090
It gives you the corresponding
Unicode code point

00:27:58.090 --> 00:27:59.537
as a string, so to speak.

00:27:59.537 --> 00:28:00.620
And that's very confusing.

00:28:00.620 --> 00:28:02.938
And that was there just
for historic reasons,

00:28:02.938 --> 00:28:04.730
because it was convenient
in the beginning.

00:28:04.730 --> 00:28:07.050
And that's something
we want to take out,

00:28:07.050 --> 00:28:09.977
but we can't, because it would
break potentially things.

00:28:09.977 --> 00:28:11.810
And there's a lot of
other things, probably.

00:28:11.810 --> 00:28:14.733
I don't have a good list
in my head at the moment.

00:28:14.733 --> 00:28:16.400
BRAD FITZPATRICK:
Ranging over a string,

00:28:16.400 --> 00:28:19.015
giving you the UTF-8
code points rather than

00:28:19.015 --> 00:28:20.390
the bytes of the
string, which is

00:28:20.390 --> 00:28:22.580
inconsistent with
the slice of bytes.

00:28:22.580 --> 00:28:26.060
There's things like
when you do a range loop

00:28:26.060 --> 00:28:28.370
and you want to capture,
you have a closure that

00:28:28.370 --> 00:28:29.930
captures one of the variables.

00:28:29.930 --> 00:28:32.030
The variables are just the same
variable for the whole loop,

00:28:32.030 --> 00:28:33.720
rather than every
iteration of the loop.

00:28:33.720 --> 00:28:35.690
So you can't safely
start a Go routine

00:28:35.690 --> 00:28:38.170
with a closure in the
middle of the loop.

00:28:38.170 --> 00:28:39.610
And hat bites a lot of people.

00:28:39.610 --> 00:28:40.985
So there's a lot
of little things

00:28:40.985 --> 00:28:45.406
that we realized after the fact,
but we can't really change it.

00:28:45.406 --> 00:28:47.630
IAN TAYLOR: I would get rid
of return statements that

00:28:47.630 --> 00:28:49.547
don't list the values
that are being returned.

00:28:53.118 --> 00:28:54.660
ROBERT GRIESEMER:
From the beginning,

00:28:54.660 --> 00:28:59.930
we tried to make Go really
platform-independent.

00:28:59.930 --> 00:29:02.760
And so most of the language is
actually platform-independent.

00:29:02.760 --> 00:29:05.260
You can write some code, and
you can run it on any platform.

00:29:05.260 --> 00:29:09.350
But some basic things, like
the integer type int or uint,

00:29:09.350 --> 00:29:12.290
they are platform-dependent.

00:29:12.290 --> 00:29:15.410
If you were running on a 32-bit
platform, you get a 32-bit int.

00:29:15.410 --> 00:29:18.090
And if you're running
on a 64-bit platform,

00:29:18.090 --> 00:29:24.350
you're getting potentially a
64-bit int, maybe a 32-bit int.

00:29:24.350 --> 00:29:27.320
So that's actually
something that I think,

00:29:27.320 --> 00:29:29.510
in retrospect, was
probably a mistake.

00:29:29.510 --> 00:29:32.780
Because if you actually move
from one platform to another,

00:29:32.780 --> 00:29:34.642
you might incur
problems, or you may

00:29:34.642 --> 00:29:36.350
have to change code
and things like that.

00:29:36.350 --> 00:29:39.120
So it would be nice if we had
had a better solution for that.

00:29:39.120 --> 00:29:40.940
And one approach
could be, or could

00:29:40.940 --> 00:29:46.155
have been, maybe, that an int is
arbitrary size and the compiler

00:29:46.155 --> 00:29:47.030
does the right thing.

00:29:47.030 --> 00:29:49.500
And you never have
to worry about it.

00:29:49.500 --> 00:29:51.560
That's something that
would be much, much harder,

00:29:51.560 --> 00:29:53.477
or is going to be much,
much harder to do now.

00:29:55.152 --> 00:29:57.360
MEGAN SANICKI: Well, we only
have a few minutes left.

00:29:57.360 --> 00:29:58.890
So we've got a few questions.

00:29:58.890 --> 00:30:00.040
People are queuing up.

00:30:00.040 --> 00:30:03.250
So we'll see if we can go
quickly and answer some more

00:30:03.250 --> 00:30:03.750
questions.

00:30:03.750 --> 00:30:04.020
AUDIENCE: Sure.

00:30:04.020 --> 00:30:05.760
Just really quickly,
what would you

00:30:05.760 --> 00:30:09.000
say has been the
biggest challenge

00:30:09.000 --> 00:30:11.490
in creating Go, or
just a programming

00:30:11.490 --> 00:30:13.240
language in general?

00:30:13.240 --> 00:30:15.510
Is it the intricate
technicalities?

00:30:15.510 --> 00:30:19.230
Or is it understanding user
behavior and creating something

00:30:19.230 --> 00:30:21.075
to adapt to user behavior?

00:30:24.690 --> 00:30:27.173
IAN TAYLOR: So what's been
the biggest challenge in Go?

00:30:27.173 --> 00:30:29.340
I'd say that, for me at
least, the biggest challenge

00:30:29.340 --> 00:30:30.990
is keeping it simple.

00:30:30.990 --> 00:30:32.940
It's always so easy to
add another feature.

00:30:32.940 --> 00:30:35.400
And you can see so
clearly how useful

00:30:35.400 --> 00:30:38.730
it would be to have this new
facility in the language.

00:30:38.730 --> 00:30:42.000
But if we add every new
facility in the language,

00:30:42.000 --> 00:30:43.680
then we get something
so complicated

00:30:43.680 --> 00:30:46.620
that it's very difficult to use,
like some existing languages

00:30:46.620 --> 00:30:47.340
today.

00:30:47.340 --> 00:30:50.670
And so I'd say the biggest
challenge has been to say no.

00:30:54.290 --> 00:30:56.180
MEGAN SANICKI: OK, over here.

00:30:56.180 --> 00:30:57.080
AUDIENCE: Hi, again.

00:30:57.080 --> 00:31:01.540
I have read a blog post,
the Golang official blog,

00:31:01.540 --> 00:31:03.490
that talks about a context.

00:31:03.490 --> 00:31:06.230
And it says that
internally in Google, it's

00:31:06.230 --> 00:31:09.070
mandatory to pass the
context in the first argument

00:31:09.070 --> 00:31:11.540
in every function.

00:31:11.540 --> 00:31:14.100
First of all, I wanted to know,
what do you think about it?

00:31:14.100 --> 00:31:16.430
And also, I've read on
the internet somewhere

00:31:16.430 --> 00:31:20.930
that context will be removed
in Go 2 or something.

00:31:20.930 --> 00:31:23.240
I don't know if it's true.

00:31:23.240 --> 00:31:25.580
BRAD FITZPATRICK: There are
no plans to remove context.

00:31:25.580 --> 00:31:29.090
If anything, it will
become more pervasive.

00:31:29.090 --> 00:31:32.690
AUDIENCE: And what about
the mandatory thing of

00:31:32.690 --> 00:31:35.190
passing the context
in the first argument?

00:31:35.190 --> 00:31:38.270
Do you think everybody
should do it outside Google?

00:31:38.270 --> 00:31:39.980
BRAD FITZPATRICK:
Yeah, probably.

00:31:39.980 --> 00:31:43.490
So inside Google, a
lot of it was implicit,

00:31:43.490 --> 00:31:46.380
with magic ThreadLocal stuff.

00:31:46.380 --> 00:31:48.320
And so it was great
when it worked,

00:31:48.320 --> 00:31:50.960
because your context was
threaded through automatically.

00:31:50.960 --> 00:31:53.090
Because all functions
just picked it up

00:31:53.090 --> 00:31:55.400
from ThreadLocal storage.

00:31:55.400 --> 00:31:58.140
But once you start to
do something tricky,

00:31:58.140 --> 00:32:01.060
a lot of people didn't
know how to stitch together

00:32:01.060 --> 00:32:02.120
their contexts.

00:32:02.120 --> 00:32:04.352
And they wouldn't even
know that they didn't do it

00:32:04.352 --> 00:32:05.810
until they had a
production problem

00:32:05.810 --> 00:32:09.773
and they went to look at
the global trace dashboard.

00:32:09.773 --> 00:32:11.690
And then they noticed
that none of their stuff

00:32:11.690 --> 00:32:13.290
was stitched together properly.

00:32:13.290 --> 00:32:15.303
So if you make it
explicit, you get

00:32:15.303 --> 00:32:16.470
it right from the beginning.

00:32:16.470 --> 00:32:18.620
So when you have a
production problem,

00:32:18.620 --> 00:32:21.335
it's usable, the traces.

00:32:21.335 --> 00:32:23.210
IAN TAYLOR: And as far
as whether the context

00:32:23.210 --> 00:32:24.680
is the first
argument or not, it's

00:32:24.680 --> 00:32:26.513
convenient to have it
be the first argument.

00:32:26.513 --> 00:32:28.970
It just makes the code be
stylistically consistent.

00:32:28.970 --> 00:32:31.340
But it doesn't have any
meaning beyond that.

00:32:31.340 --> 00:32:33.950
It just helps if the code looks
similar in different parts

00:32:33.950 --> 00:32:34.450
of Google.

00:32:34.450 --> 00:32:36.200
BRAD FITZPATRICK: One
thing I'd like to do

00:32:36.200 --> 00:32:38.390
is maybe make context.context
be less stuttery.

00:32:38.390 --> 00:32:40.190
Because right now,
you write CTX.

00:32:40.190 --> 00:32:43.460
And so if you're reading it,
it's contextcontext.context.

00:32:43.460 --> 00:32:46.820
And right now, we
have one built-in type

00:32:46.820 --> 00:32:49.400
in the language, one
built-in interface type,

00:32:49.400 --> 00:32:51.860
which is error, which is
just an alias for anything

00:32:51.860 --> 00:32:53.392
with an error string method.

00:32:53.392 --> 00:32:55.850
So it would be nice maybe just
to make context first class,

00:32:55.850 --> 00:32:57.017
too, and make that built-in.

00:32:57.017 --> 00:32:59.710
So at least then it would just
be contextcontext or something.

00:33:04.470 --> 00:33:05.220
MEGAN SANICKI: OK.

00:33:05.220 --> 00:33:09.870
So I think we can get one,
maybe two questions in.

00:33:09.870 --> 00:33:12.590
We'll start on this side.

00:33:12.590 --> 00:33:13.490
AUDIENCE: Hi.

00:33:13.490 --> 00:33:16.160
For such a new language,
I think my math is right,

00:33:16.160 --> 00:33:19.860
that there's over 50 years
of experience and knowledge

00:33:19.860 --> 00:33:21.390
just on this stage.

00:33:21.390 --> 00:33:26.742
Can we get maybe one tip from
each of you for writing in Go

00:33:26.742 --> 00:33:28.075
and getting into the Go mindset?

00:33:33.782 --> 00:33:36.240
TYLER BUI-PALSULICH: I'll start,
and I'll take an easy one.

00:33:36.240 --> 00:33:39.240
Run gofmt and
require it on submit.

00:33:39.240 --> 00:33:41.490
You should run it on Save
as you're writing your code.

00:33:41.490 --> 00:33:42.960
Same way with goimports.

00:33:45.723 --> 00:33:47.140
BRAD FITZPATRICK:
Small functions.

00:33:47.140 --> 00:33:47.920
Lots of tests.

00:33:51.360 --> 00:33:53.760
ROBERT GRIESEMER: Play
with it in the playground

00:33:53.760 --> 00:33:56.582
and experiment.

00:33:56.582 --> 00:33:58.290
ROBERT VAN GENT: Be
careful with context.

00:33:58.290 --> 00:34:00.540
I've had a lot of bugs with
using the wrong context

00:34:00.540 --> 00:34:02.648
in the wrong place.

00:34:02.648 --> 00:34:04.940
IAN TAYLOR: Be explicit about
what you're trying to do.

00:34:04.940 --> 00:34:08.270
Keep your functions.

00:34:08.270 --> 00:34:11.611
Don't use implicit information
passed between functions.

00:34:15.038 --> 00:34:16.080
MEGAN SANICKI: OK, great.

00:34:16.080 --> 00:34:17.860
We're going to sneak
in one last question,

00:34:17.860 --> 00:34:19.230
then we'll wrap it up.

00:34:19.230 --> 00:34:20.620
AUDIENCE: Hi, everyone.

00:34:20.620 --> 00:34:22.650
I have a question for Brad.

00:34:22.650 --> 00:34:26.580
You started writing the second
iteration of the Net::HTTP

00:34:26.580 --> 00:34:28.170
package.

00:34:28.170 --> 00:34:30.060
Can you talk a little
bit about the problems

00:34:30.060 --> 00:34:32.550
you have with your
original iteration of it

00:34:32.550 --> 00:34:34.582
and maybe what the status is?

00:34:34.582 --> 00:34:36.540
BRAD FITZPATRICK: Well,
I have a long Wiki page

00:34:36.540 --> 00:34:40.080
about all the things I dislike
about the first one, which

00:34:40.080 --> 00:34:42.300
grew organically
over 10 years or so.

00:34:42.300 --> 00:34:45.159
So I don't really have time
in 30 seconds to go over that.

00:34:45.159 --> 00:34:48.239
But the current status
is, I got distracted.

00:34:48.239 --> 00:34:50.010
There were other
fires and stuff.

00:34:50.010 --> 00:34:51.130
It's still my goal.

00:34:51.130 --> 00:34:52.590
It's probably the
highest priority

00:34:52.590 --> 00:34:54.420
thing I should be
doing, is finishing up

00:34:54.420 --> 00:34:57.000
the new HTTP client.

00:34:57.000 --> 00:35:00.100
IAN TAYLOR: We're all
looking forward to it.

00:35:00.100 --> 00:35:01.030
MEGAN SANICKI: OK.

00:35:01.030 --> 00:35:02.090
Well, we're out of time.

00:35:02.090 --> 00:35:04.420
But thank you so much
for joining us and all

00:35:04.420 --> 00:35:06.130
your great questions.

00:35:06.130 --> 00:35:09.190
And all the credit goes to these
great experts and the community

00:35:09.190 --> 00:35:10.000
that's building Go.

00:35:10.000 --> 00:35:13.350
[MUSIC PLAYING]

